  "notes": "The definitive pop-punk producer/mixer — \"the reason the lane sounds punchy and analogue rather than clinical\". 140–190 straight, backbeat on 2&4 at speed, and the harmonic policy on his best-known record is documented: C major, C–F–G, \"**nothing else**\", at 150 BPM, plus vi–IV–I–V. So three short families, a triad share of 0.8, and `maxChords` 3. The drum philosophy is the entry's substance and it is all about tone rather than pattern: he considered drum recording \"a lost art\", kept **large snare collections to A/B tone per song**, and favoured **room mics over close-miking every component**. A note generator hears that as a played kit, so this is the one place `humanize` opens on a pop-punk model — a real velocity spread and per-lane jitter — with the accent tier loud and the ghost band wider than Blink's ghost-free reading, which is how these two models separate. The other deltas are quoted: \"kick 8ths under the chorus\", \"hats 8ths in verse, crash-8ths in chorus\", and \"fills are one-bar 16th snare/tom runs\". His compression signature — **slow attack, fast release**, \"keeping transients while letting go instantly\" — is why the accent tier is wide rather than squashed. The bass is \"tracked bright and picky, root 8ths locked to the guitar; bass alone carries the verse when the guitars drop\". ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are every drum probability and the velocity tiers; the drum philosophy, guitar method, compression settings and a flagship record's parameters are sourced, the values are not.",
